  "worked_solution": "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{2}=10\\,\u27f9\\,{c}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot 2=5\\,\u27f9\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
